From: tburns@mason1.gmu.edu (T. David Burns)
Date: Thu, 5 May 1994 00:35:54 -0500
Subject: [#94-5-57] ECON: The limits to comparative advantage

At  8:54 AM 5/4/94 -0400, Peter C. McCluskey wrote:
> Comparative advantage is a good argument against protectionist barriers
>between regions whose productivity differs by a few orders of magnitude
>at most, but fails when applied to really different regions.
> Suppose that in the era of high robot productivity, interest rates have
>risen to 10%/hour (hardly an unusual assumption if most economic processes
>have been speeded up by a few orders of magnitude). Now imagine that a
>robotic factory can transform $1.00 worth of rock into $1.10 worth of
>widgets in 0.99999 hours (eeking out a tiny profit), but that replacing
>any part of the manufacturing process with human labor would slow down
>the process so that it took more than 1 hour produce the $1.10 in widgets.
>If this phenomenon is sufficiently widespread, then nobody in his right mind
>would invest in anything that uses a human to do anything that a robot could.

This argument gets around comparative advantage by banning scarcity. The
classic tale of comparative advantage involves two countries (or two
people). I think I saw David Friedman use the example of the doctor and the
doctor's secretary. Suppose the doctor is not only more skilled in medicine
than the secretary is, but also is a faster typer, a more skilled
organizer, better at filing. This doctor is a real wiz. It still pays the
doctor to hire the secretary to do the typing (more slowly, with more
errors) rather than do it herself, since time spent typing could be spent
more productively doctoring. The doctor gets an opportunity cost wage, an
implicit wage, for any typing she does. While this wage may be higher than
the secretary's, it's lower than the income from doctoring. So in the
ideal, the doctor does no typing, does more doctoring than she would if she
had to type, pays the secretary with the some of the difference, and still
has some left - she comes out ahead. So in the case of the robots, so long
as the robots are not equally productive in every task, it pays to
concentrate them in the more productive tasks and pay humans to do the
others.

This doesn't work if there is no scarcity. If the doctor has infinite time
available, or we can make as many copies of the doctor as we want, it's
more efficient to let her type things herself, since it's cheaper and
higher quality. This is what Peter has done with the robots. Every new task
which presents itself gets a robot assigned to it. If there are more robots
than tasks, and robots are able to complete all tasks at a very low cost,
this works. There are no niches left for humans in this environment.

Economists, being what they are, assume scarcity - that there are always
more tasks than robots, always at least one more thing someone would rather
have done than not have done, and hence some positive real wage at which
humans can gain employment. Of course, coming up with those tasks may
strain our imagination - but then we can always assign a robot to figure
out what needs to be done by humans.

