 tburns@mason1.gmu.edu  (T. David Burns) writes in X-Message-Number: #94-5-57:
>This doesn't work if there is no scarcity. If the doctor has infinite time
>available, or we can make as many copies of the doctor as we want, it's
>more efficient to let her type things herself, since it's cheaper and
>higher quality. This is what Peter has done with the robots. Every new task
>which presents itself gets a robot assigned to it. If there are more robots
>than tasks, and robots are able to complete all tasks at a very low cost,
>this works. There are no niches left for humans in this environment.

 While I was claiming that the value of labor would drop to a very low
level compared to the value of capital, my argument does not depend on
it going all the way to zero. I believe that the value of robotic labor
would remain slightly positive because copying a robot will require some
time and raw materials. Differences in the quality of labor can still
keep the value of human labor below zero (employing human labor would
cause an increase in the cost of capital because the capital would be
tied up longer, or the cost of verifying the correctness of the output
might exceed the value of the labor).

 brauchfu@pcocd2.intel.com (Brian Rauchfuss - PCD) writes in X-Message-Number: #94-5-46:
>>  Suppose that in the era of high robot productivity, interest rates have
>> risen to 10%/hour (hardly an unusual assumption if most economic processes
>> have been speeded up by a few orders of magnitude). Now imagine that a
