>This seems like an unusual assumption to me, since interest rates did nothing
>of the sort during the industrial revolution, the last time economic processes
>increased by a few orders of magnitude.  Interest rates increased, but only
>by a factor of 2 to 3. Real interest rates moved by even less, since inflation
>accounts for part of the apparent move. (See the _Wall Street Waltz_ by Fisher
>for a variety of interesting historical data).

 It is probably true that real interest rates haven't risen over the past
few centuries (the uncertanties in how to adjust for inflation and risk of
default limit the usefullness of this comparison). Interest rates appear
to have some relation to the growth rate now. I am not sure whether that
was true before the industrial revolution.
 I think that so far the value of capital has been limited by the need for
labor to make that capital useful. I think that sometime in the next
century that will change to the point where the main limit on the value
of capital will be physical limits on the speed at which resources get
moved around, and that this transition will control the increase in rates.

