From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
Date: Thu, 5 May 1994 12:09:22 -0700
Subject: [#94-5-63] ECON: The limits to comparative advantage

Peter C. McCluskey writes:
>Hans Moravec was criticized at Extro 1 for ignoring the principle
>of comparative advantage in his science fiction story of a time when
>robotic slaves would eliminate all human jobs. ... But his belief that
>the value of most human labor would go to zero seems very reasonable
>to me.  

This could be a good topic for discussion, but I'd be much comfortable
if we had more precise claims to discuss.  Does "eliminate all human
jobs" mean that all humans don't work?  That most don't?  "value ...
goes to zero" relative to what?  (Value isn't measured in inches you know.)

For example, if one imagines that each human had enough
(non-human-capital) wealth to buy a robot slave on average 100 times
as productive as that human (ranging say from 80x on some tasks to
1000x on others), they it is likely that few people would work except
for the joy of work.

>Comparative advantage is a good argument against protectionist
>barriers between regions whose productivity differs by a few orders of
>magnitude at most, but fails when applied to really different regions.

I really don't see how the order of magnitude is relevant.

T. David Burns responds:
>This argument gets around comparative advantage by banning scarcity.
>... So in the case of the robots, so long as the robots are not
>equally productive in every task, it pays to concentrate them in the
>more productive tasks and pay humans to do the others. ...
>Economists, being what they are, assume scarcity - that there are
>always more tasks than robots, always at least one more thing someone
>would rather have done than not have done, and hence some positive
>real wage at which humans can gain employment.

This argument does not guarentee that this wage will be high enough
for the human to live on, if the price of food, etc. is influenced by
robot demand.  Also, if the production function that includes human
labor also requires non-zero amounts of other inputs, inputs with an
opportunity cost for use by a robot production function, then there
might be zero use of human labor in equilibrium.  Imagine a robot
doctor wouldn't mind a human secretary, but sure as hell can't afford
to allocate a square inch of office space for such a slow beast, or
even spending more than a millisecond's thought to decide which one to
hire, evaluate their work, etc.

Robin Hanson

